{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:MassTheory.EigenvalueProductExpansion","@type":"LeanModule","name":"MassTheory.EigenvalueProductExpansion","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Group.NatPowAssoc","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.Field.GeomSum","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.Ring.GeomSum","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Ring.Regular","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Log.Basic"],"declarations":4,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.four_term_expansion","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.four_term_expansion","kind":"theorem","line":58,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/eigenvalue-product-expansion.lean#L58-L73","doc":"The four-term expansion of `ln|Λ_sym|` is exact:\n\n  `ln|Λ_sym| = ℓ₂·A²/4 + ℓ·T·A - ℓ·T - ℓ₂·T²`\n\nwhere `A = Z+N`, `T = (Z-N)/2`, `ℓ = ln q`, `ℓ₂ = ln([2]/2)`.  This\nfollows from the substitution `Z = A/2+T`, `N = A/2-T` into\n`n_pp - n_nn = T(A-1)` and `n_pn = A²/4 - T²`.\n\nStated here as the `ℝ`-coefficient quadratic identity that the\nsubstitution produces (with the real exponents `t, a` standing for\n`T, A`); the `ℓ`, `ℓ₂` are carried as the log coefficients and the\nidentity is the algebraic core proved by `ring`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.geometric_sum_is_qint","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.geometric_sum_is_qint","kind":"theorem","line":43,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/eigenvalue-product-expansion.lean#L43-L56","doc":"**q-integer as geometric sum.**  The `q`-integer `[c+1]_q` is the\ngeometric sum of eigenvalue powers over `c` crossings.  After clearing\nthe negative powers (multiply the displayed identity\n`Σ_{j=0}^c q^{c-2j} = (q^{c+1}-q^{-(c+1)})/(q-q⁻¹)` through by `q^c`),\nthe content is the real geometric-series identity in `x = q²`:\n\n  `(∑_{k=0}^{c} (q²)^k) · (q² - 1) = (q²)^{c+1} - 1`,\n\ni.e. `Σ_{k=0}^{c} q^{2k} = (q^{2(c+1)} - 1)/(q² - 1)`.  This is the\n`[c+1]_q` quantum-integer identity, here **derived** from\n`geom_sum_mul` rather than asserted. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.crossing_exponent","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.crossing_exponent","kind":"theorem","line":34,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/eigenvalue-product-expansion.lean#L34-L41","doc":"The crossing-count exponent numerator identity\n`Z(Z-1) - N(N-1) = (Z-N)(Z+N-1)` (the `×2`, division-free form of\n`n_pp - n_nn = (Z-N)(A-1)/2`; both numerators are even, so the halved\nidentity follows, but the polynomial identity is the genuine algebraic\ncontent and is proved here by `ring`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.symEigenvalue","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.symEigenvalue","kind":"def","line":26,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/eigenvalue-product-expansion.lean#L26-L32","doc":"The symmetric channel eigenvalue for each crossing type. ","signatureChunk":0}]}